          Programs that fault, and the trap of testing them bare          ======================================================A program that stops with    Exit code: E_PRCABT(228) Process Aborted    Exception: pid=2 vector=$02 err=#000:102has taken a bus error -- vector $02 on a 68000: the program read an addressthe machine would not give it.  os9exec kills the process, so nothing isprinted and the program looks simply dead.  `aterm` and `snake` did thathere, and both are fine.READ THIS BEFORE BELIEVING ANY OF IT------------------------------------A program run as os9exec's FIRST process has no environment at all -- no TERM,no TERMCAP, no PATH.  Nothing on this disk is used that way.  `SYS/login` iswhat sets TERM and TERMCAP, and 69 programs here read TERMCAP.Run bare, `aterm` and `snake` took bus errors and `cribbage` and `crib`printed "Environment variable TERM not defined" and aborted.  Run inside alogin session -- `bash /dd/SYS/login`, then the program -- all four work:`aterm` prints its banner, `snake` draws its board, and both cribbages askwhether you would like the instructions.So the same missing TERMCAP surfaces as a clean error message in one programand as a bus error in another, depending on whether its terminal librarychecks the pointer before following it.  A bus error is not evidence that aprogram is broken.  It may only be evidence that you ran it wrong.Host environment variables do not help: setting TERM in the shell that startsos9exec changes nothing, because os9exec builds the OS-9 environment itself.Going through `SYS/login` is the only way.One that looked like it wanted floating-point hardware, and did not-------------------------------------------------------------------This section used to say that two programs execute floating-pointinstructions with no coprocessor underneath.  One of them is not aprogram, and the other was not doing that:  os9lib    is the RTF Fortran RUN-TIME LIBRARY, not a program.  Running a            library AS a program is what reached its floating-point code.            See DOC/README-FORTRAN; it is not a failure.  config    printed char, short, int, long, pointer and FLOAT properties            correctly, then aborted on vector $0B -- the 68000's line-F            emulator vector, which is how a coprocessor instruction            announces that there is no coprocessor.  It died where            `double` began.  IT RUNS TO ITS LAST LINE NOW.THE VECTOR SAID COPROCESSOR AND THE CAUSE WAS SOMETHING ELSE ENTIRELY.`config` INSTALLS ITS OWN HANDLER for that vector: it means to catch theexception and do the arithmetic in software, which is exactly why itneeds no coprocessor.  The handler was going in at the wrong address.  AnF$STrap table gives each handler as a word offset from the table entry,SIGNED, reaching plus or minus 32K -- so a table placed after the routinesit names carries a negative one -- and reading that word unsigned puts thehandler 64K above the routine.  The process then dies at the veryexception it asked to be told about.  Corrected in the emulator on2026-09-12, and measured here by running `config` on the build before thatchange and the build with it.SO THE LESSON IS THE ONE THIS FILE OPENS WITH.  A fault on the vector forthe very thing a program is doing is not evidence that the hardware ismissing, any more than a bus error is evidence that a program is broken.A program that installs a handler and then dies on that handler's ownvector is a program whose handler did not go where it asked.For the record, since this was written down as a reason to ask for more:Microware's `fpu` module is the floating-point emulator for a machine withno 68881, and `p2init` is what installs it as a system extension.  Thepermission this collection asked for and received covers cio, math,math881, csl and csl020, and NOTHING HERE NEEDS MORE THAN THAT -- `config`was the concrete case for asking about `fpu`, and it turned out not to beone.Five SNOBOL4 games -- FIXED 2026-08-19, they play------------------------------------------------  poker  blackjak  rpoem  rstory  stoneThese faulted with `Illegal instruction: 4afc', which is both the 68000ILLEGAL opcode and the OS-9 module sync word -- so control had jumped intoa module header.  That looked like a corrupt library and was not.Each opens a SYNTAX FILE by a bare relative name (PHRASE.SYN, RPOEM.SYN,RSTORY.SYN, RSTORY.ACT).  The open failed, the null result was used as apattern tree, and the first match call jumped through a null functionpointer.  The files existed all along, in DOC/snobol, which is notsomewhere a running program looks.They now live in GAMES/SNOBOL and the five were rebuilt fromSRC/effo_snobol to name them there.  A `chd' would not have been enough:bash cannot change the OS-9 data directory and sh cannot fork an absolutepathname, so no user could have satisfied the old relative open.`tformat' always worked because it does not use the pattern matcher andopens no syntax file.  So did `rstory2', which was dropped from thecollection on 2026-09-12 for an unrelated reason: the four story programsit forks were never distributed.About aterm-----------`aterm` works.  It is 100% 68000 assembly, and the collection now includesits configuration:    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Ctl     control file, 20 sequences, ANSI/VT-100 codes    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Phone   empty dialler list, ready to fill in    SYS/ATERM/ATerm.Print   printer scriptThe control file was written from the example in the program's own manual, withthe VT-52 escape sequences of the original replaced by ANSI ones and the devicedriver name set to `term`.  `DOC/aterm/ATerm.Doc` is the full manual and`SRC/aterm/ATerm.a` the complete source.  To do anything useful it still wantsa real serial port: `aterm /t1`.